## Reason About the Unselected Branches



On code S, the express test fails and the standard branch supplies 3. The pickup and invalid alternatives do not execute. Trace statements that actually run, not every statement printed on the page. If you initialize the charge to zero and omit invalid handling, an unknown code may accidentally receive the pickup charge. A plausible value is not evidence that a category was recognized.

Guided variation: add code D for digital delivery at a charge of 1. Update the decision table, predict D and an unknown code, then update both selection forms. Explain why the unknown code must still be distinguishable from pickup.

```{dropdown} Reasoning checkpoint
D becomes another supported alternative. The invalid case remains necessary because expanding the set of valid codes does not make every string valid. Keep either a separate success flag or an explicit invalid branch instead of treating the initial charge as proof of successful classification.
```


using System;
string service = "S";
int charge;
switch (service)
{
    case "E": charge = 8; break;
    case "S": charge = 3; break;
    case "P": charge = 0; break;
    default: Console.WriteLine("Unknown service"); return;
}
Console.WriteLine($"Service {service}: {charge}");


