## `else-if` Statements for Cases




A numerical score maps to one letter grade. The cutoffs for A, B, C, and D are 90, 80, 70, and 60. First examine a nested version, then compare it with a flat `else-if` chain.


double score = 72.5;
    char letter;
    if (score >= 90) {
        letter = 'A';
    }
    else {   // grade must be B, C, D or F
        if (score >= 80) {
            letter = 'B';
        }
        else { // grade must be C, D or F
            if (score >= 70) {
            letter = 'C';
            }
            else {   // grade must D or F
            if (score >= 60) {
                letter = 'D';
            }
            else {
                letter = 'F';
            }
            }   //end else D or F
        }      // end of else C, D, or F
    }         // end of else B, C, D or F
Console.WriteLine(letter);  // C


The nested version keeps increasing the indentation even though the result is one of several peer categories. An `else-if` chain expresses those categories more clearly. Each condition is tested only if every preceding condition was false.

`if (`


*condition1*


`) {`

statement-block-run-if-condition1-is-true;

`}`

`else if (`


*condition2*


`) {`

statement-block-run-if-condition2-is-the-first-true;

`}`

`else if (`


*condition3*


`) {`

statement-block-run-if-condition3-is-the-first-true;

`}`

// ...

`else {    //`


*no condition!*

statement-block-run-if-no-condition-is-true;

`}`

Exactly one branch executes: the first true condition selects its branch; if none is true, the final `else` executes. Compare the following version using the same score.


double score = 72.5;
    char letter;
    if (score >= 90) {
        letter = 'A';
    }
    else if (score >= 80) {
        letter = 'B';
        }
    else if (score >= 70) {
        letter = 'C';
    }
    else if (score >= 60) {
        letter = 'D';
    }
    else {
        letter = 'F';
    }
Console.WriteLine(letter);  // C


