## String Operations




As introduced in the preceding `for` lesson, `text.Length` supplies a string's length and `text[i]` reads one zero-based `char`. Use an index below the length before accessing a character. The following program prints a fixed text one character per line; no helper method or array is needed.


string text = "Order";
int i = 0;
while (i < text.Length)
{
    Console.WriteLine(text[i]);
    i++;
}



We are using `(i < text.Length)` because we want to loop through all the characters.
Since string indexing is 0-based, the index of the last character is the
*length of the string - 1*. In the code here, it is text.Length - 1. To represent this
number, we use:



```csharp
while (i < text.Length) {
```


To print out the characters, we loop through the string use the string indexes `text[i]`:



string text = "Order";
int i = 2;
Console.WriteLine(text[i]);  // d



To print out the characters one by one, we update the local loop variable `i`
with step of 1:



int i = 10;
i = i+1;



or the more commonly used equivalent form:



int i = 10;
i++;


