```{index} Tree Shape and Library Choice
```

## Tree Shape and Library Choice




```{index} Tree Shape and Library Choice
```


Inserting already sorted keys into a plain binary search tree can create a chain. Its height becomes n-1 edges, so a lookup can take O(n) comparisons. Balanced search trees maintain a height proportional to log n by restructuring after updates; learning the tradeoff does not require implementing rotations in this foundation course. Choose SortedSet<T> for unique ordered values or SortedDictionary<TKey,TValue> for ordered key/value reports. Choose a hash collection when exact-key membership matters and sorted iteration is unnecessary. Measure the workload and check the contract rather than assuming every type called a tree has the same height guarantee.


using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
SortedSet<int> dueDates = new SortedSet<int>();
foreach (int day in new[] { 30, 10, 20, 20 }) dueDates.Add(day);
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", dueDates));
Console.WriteLine(dueDates.Contains(20));
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", dueDates.GetViewBetween(10, 20)));


```{index} Summary
```

## Summary




```{index} Summary
```


A BST adds an ordering invariant to a binary tree. Search and insertion follow one path when the tree is well shaped, but sorted insertion can produce a tall chain.

```{rubric} Footnotes
```


