# Shell Sort and Gap Families


```{contents}
:local:
:depth: 2
```


```{index} Each Gap Pass Solves an Interleaved Insertion Problem
```



```{index} Each Gap Pass Solves an Interleaved Insertion Problem
```

## Each Gap Pass Solves an Interleaved Insertion Problem



Shell sort connects to insertion sort by ordering positions separated by a chosen gap. With gap three, positions zero, three, six form one subsequence; positions one, four, seven form another. A pass applies insertion-like work within each subsequence. It does not claim the whole array is already sorted after that pass.

As gaps shrink, long-distance disorder can be reduced before the final gap-one insertion pass. That final pass is necessary for ordinary total ordering; being ordered at gap three alone does not compare adjacent positions from different subsequences. A gap schedule must eventually reach one under the implementation's contract.

Different schedules change which distances are addressed and how much work is performed. The retained powers-of-two, 3h+1 and 2h+1 variants are distinct experiments, not interchangeable labels for one verified complexity theorem. Measure the same input shapes and count the same operations before drawing a conclusion about a family. A successful sort on one array establishes neither a universal cost bound nor a universal best gap schedule.

Guided trace: label positions zero through seven and draw the three gap-three subsequences. Then draw the single gap-one sequence. Explain why a swap or shift across a large gap may reduce later local work while still changing the relative order of equal-key records. Shell sort should not be assumed stable simply because its inner pass resembles a stable insertion implementation.

For related explanation, see the further reading below.[^development-reading]


```{index} Shell Sort and Quicksort
```

<span id="shell-sort-and-quicksort"></span>


This supporting lesson restores the advanced algorithms from the earlier CS1 reference in the current advanced sorting chapter. Read insertion sort and recursion first. It supplements the existing searching, elementary-sort, and workload lessons.

These explanations and tasks retain the earlier CS1 teaching material.[^2304_advanced_sorts-restored]


```{index} Shell Sort
```

## Shell Sort




```{index} Shell Sort
```

Shell sort applies insertion sorting to subsequences whose elements are separated by a gap. For gap four, the sequences start at positions zero, one, two, and three, visiting every fourth element. A distant exchange can remove several inversions, moving an out-of-place value toward its destination more quickly than adjacent exchanges alone.

A single gap does not necessarily sort the whole array. Repeat with decreasing gaps, ending at one; the final pass is ordinary insertion sort over the entire array. After a gap-four pass, each gap-four subsequence is sorted, but neighboring values can still be out of order. Trace this distinction before the final pass.

The earlier lesson compared gaps 8, 4, 2, 1 with a sequence based on `h = 3*h + 1`, giving 1, 4, 13, 40, 121, and so on. Generate useful gaps for the actual array length and apply them in reverse order. The maximum gap and recurrence both affect performance; do not infer a universal complexity bound from one timing experiment. The simple halving/power-of-two family can still require quadratic work in the worst case.

```{index} Gap Experiments
```

### Gap Experiments




```{index} Gap Experiments
```

Trace the subsequences for gaps 4, 2, and 1 on `{9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1}`. Then compare `{8, 4, 2, 1}` with `{13, 4, 1}` on copies of the same longer input. Include sorted, reverse-sorted, random, duplicate-heavy, empty, and one-element arrays. Count comparisons and swaps separately. All sequences must contain a final gap one. Verify the output against a separately sorted copy before discussing efficiency.

```{index} Compare Correct Results
```

## Compare Correct Results




```{index} Compare Correct Results
```

The cell provides runnable implementations of the restored algorithms. ShellSort takes positive gaps in descending order; require a final one when sorting arrays of at least two elements. QuickSort takes a valid inclusive subrange, with an empty range represented by `low > high`. Add argument validation to a public-facing library wrapper rather than hiding invalid bounds inside the algorithm.

A useful experiment reports the gap sequence, input distribution, size, seed, comparison count, swap count, and correctness result. Timing can extend that evidence, but a single fast trial does not establish the asymptotic behavior. Continue with the search-performance lab for the difference between operation counts and measured elapsed time.

```{index} The Additional 2h Plus One Gap Sequence
```

### The Additional 2h Plus One Gap Sequence




```{index} The Additional 2h Plus One Gap Sequence
```

The original lesson also considered the recurrence `h0 = 1`, `hNext = 2 * h + 1`, giving `1, 3, 7, 15, 31, ...`. Retain it alongside powers of two and `3 * h + 1`. Build gaps smaller than the array length, then apply them in descending order, ending with one. Empty and single-element arrays need no passes.

For length 50, compare the descending sequences `32,16,8,4,2,1`, `40,13,4,1`, and `31,15,7,3,1`. The recurrence does not prove one family universally faster. Use copies of the same input, verify all results, then compare comparisons, moves, and timing separately. Avoid inferring complexity from one run or a fixed historical machine's timings.

The demonstration restores the additional family and checks its result against the other two and Array.Sort. The generator checks its arithmetic before multiplying so a large requested size does not overflow the gap calculation.

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
static int[] Gaps(int n, int multiplier) {
    List<int> gaps = new List<int>();
    for (int h = 1; h < n;) {
        gaps.Add(h);
        if (h > (n - 2) / multiplier) break;
        h = multiplier * h + 1;
    }
    gaps.Reverse(); return gaps.ToArray();
}
static int[] PowerTwoGaps(int n) {
    List<int> gaps = new List<int>();
    for (int h = 1; h < n;) {
        gaps.Add(h); if (h > (n - 1) / 2) break; h *= 2;
    }
    gaps.Reverse(); return gaps.ToArray();
}
static void ShellSort(int[] data, int[] gaps) {
    foreach (int gap in gaps)
        for (int j = gap; j < data.Length; j++) {
            int value = data[j], i = j;
            while (i >= gap && data[i - gap] > value) { data[i] = data[i - gap]; i -= gap; }
            data[i] = value;
        }
}
int[] data = new int[50]; for (int i = 0; i < data.Length; i++) data[i] = 50 - i;
int[] expected = (int[])data.Clone(); Array.Sort(expected);
int[][] families = {PowerTwoGaps(data.Length), Gaps(data.Length, 3), Gaps(data.Length, 2)};
foreach (int[] gaps in families) {
    int[] copy = (int[])data.Clone(); ShellSort(copy, gaps);
    bool correct = true; for (int i = 0; i < copy.Length; i++) if (copy[i] != expected[i]) correct = false;
    Console.WriteLine($"{string.Join(",", gaps)}: sorted={correct}");
}

